  "response": "National Weather Service local forecast offices conduct post-event damage surveys after severe storms, and only after that fieldwork is complete do the survey teams rate tornado intensity using the Enhanced Fujita Scale. The order matters. Intensity ratings are assigned to damage that has already been documented on the ground, not inferred from radar or from the impressions of residents. The Enhanced Fujita Scale was implemented in the United States in February 2007, whereas the Enhanced Fujita Scale replaced the original Fujita Scale in 1999. Its structure reflects the engineering basis that distinguishes it from its predecessor. Specifically, the Enhanced Fujita Scale uses 15 damage indicators, each paired with a sequence of degrees of damage. A surveyor matches what is observed on a structure or a stand of trees to the appropriate indicator and degree, and from that pairing derives an estimated wind speed. This procedure deliberately anchors the rating to physical evidence rather than to testimony.\n\nWhen eyewitness accounts, radar signatures, and debris tell different stories, surveyors give the debris field the greatest weight because it records the wind's direction at each point it crossed. Convergent debris patterns are characteristic of tornado damage: trees, crops, and structural fragments are laid down pointing inward toward a central track, often with a rotational or crisscrossing signature where winds from opposing directions acted in quick succession. By contrast, divergent or parallel debris patterns are characteristic of downburst damage, in which a column of descending air strikes the surface and spreads outward, flattening objects in a fan or in long rows aligned with the outflow. Surveyors map these orientations, increasingly with drone and satellite imagery, so that the vector field of the damage becomes visible across the whole affected area rather than at isolated points.\n\nWidth is the second discriminating parameter. Tornado damage paths are typically narrower than downburst damage swaths, and because of that difference in scale the damage swath was half a mile wide. That is, it measured 0.5 miles across, a dimension that falls comfortably within the range of a downburst outflow but that would require an unusually large tornado. Surveyors then compare that width against the debris orientation along its full length, looking for any narrower corridor of convergent damage embedded within the broader swath. Radar is consulted at this stage as well: a velocity couplet aloft indicates rotation in the parent storm, but it does not by itself establish that the circulation reached the ground, and a strong reflectivity core with a divergent velocity signature near the surface points instead to outflow.\n\nIn the case at issue, at least one of two events occurred, perhaps both: a brief tornado touched down within the damage area, or a downburst struck the damage area. Eyewitnesses described a funnel, radar showed weak rotation, and a short segment of the debris field displayed convergence, while the dominant pattern across the swath was divergent. The classification decision is binary for any given segment of the path. Either the survey team classified the storm damage as caused by a tornado, or, despite that brief touchdown, it classified the storm damage as caused by straight-line winds; the two cannot both be the classification, and one of them must be chosen. The forecast office's meteorologist-in-charge determined that the survey team's official classification would be straight-line winds, not a tornado.\n\nThe NWS final survey report settles that tension: it concluded that the storm damage was produced by straight-line downburst winds, and that conclusion confirms that the downburst was the event responsible for the destruction across the swath. The brief touchdown is acknowledged in the narrative record, but its contribution was too small and too localized to govern the classification of the damage as a whole. This outcome illustrates the general rule surveyors apply when evidence conflicts. Testimony and radar establish what was possible, but the orientation and dimensions of the damage itself determine what is recorded.",
